### How does Flink trigger a checkpoint?

The checkpoint coordinator lives in the JobManager and triggers a checkpoint on a configured interval. To start, it tells every source to inject a barrier with a new checkpoint ID into its output stream.

```java
import org.apache.flink.streaming.api.environment.StreamExecutionEnvironment;

StreamExecutionEnvironment env =
    StreamExecutionEnvironment.getExecutionEnvironment();

// Enable checkpointing every 10 seconds
env.enableCheckpointing(10_000);

// Configure checkpoint storage
env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointStorage(
    "s3://nbi-flink-checkpoints/");

// Set exactly-once mode
env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointingMode(
    org.apache.flink.streaming.api.CheckpointingMode.EXACTLY_ONCE);
```

```text
Checkpoint trigger flow:
  JobManager (Coordinator)
       │
       ├──▶ Source 1: inject barrier(id=42)
       ├──▶ Source 2: inject barrier(id=42)
       └──▶ Source 3: inject barrier(id=42)
```

### What are checkpoint barriers?

A barrier is not data; it is a marker that separates records belonging before the checkpoint from those belonging after it. Barriers flow downstream exactly like records, never overtaking them, so the ordering is preserved.

```java
// Conceptual stream content (barriers are injected by the source):
// [record] [record] [record] [BARRIER-42] [record] [record] ...
//          ◀── before CP-42 ──▶         ◀── after CP-42 ──▶
```

```text
Barrier flow through the graph:
  Source ──▶ [R1] [R2] [B42] [R3] ──▶ Map ──▶ [R1'] [R2'] [B42] [R3']
                                          ──▶ Sink
  Barrier B42 separates pre-CP and post-CP records
  Barrier never overtakes records: ordering preserved
```

### How does barrier alignment work?

When a multi-input operator (after a join or union) receives the barrier on one input, it stops consuming that channel and buffers further records from it, while continuing the other channels until their barriers arrive. Only then does it snapshot.

```java
// Multi-input operator: join of retail and corporate streams
// Input 1 (retail):    [R1] [R2] [B42] [R3] [R4]
// Input 2 (corporate): [C1] [C2] [C3] [C4] [B42]

// Step 1: B42 arrives on Input 1 first
//   - Stop consuming Input 1, buffer R3, R4
//   - Continue consuming Input 2: process C1, C2, C3, C4

// Step 2: B42 arrives on Input 2
//   - All barriers received
//   - Snapshot state (exactly all pre-B42 records processed)
//   - Forward B42 downstream
//   - Drain buffered R3, R4 from Input 1
```

### How does asynchronous snapshotting work?

When an operator snapshots, it captures a copy of its state and hands it to a background thread that uploads it to durable storage. The main processing thread is barely blocked.

```java
// Snapshot is asynchronous:
// 1. Operator takes a lightweight copy-on-write snapshot
// 2. Background thread serializes and uploads to S3
// 3. Processing thread continues with new records
// 4. When upload completes, operator ACKs to coordinator

// Checkpoint storage configuration:
env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointStorage(
    "s3://nbi-flink-checkpoints/job-fraud-scoring/");
// Snapshots uploaded here asynchronously
```

### How does recovery work?

On failure, Flink restarts the job, restores every operator's state from the last completed checkpoint, and rewinds the sources to the offsets recorded in that checkpoint.

```java
// Recovery steps:
// 1. Detect failure (task manager crash)
// 2. Cancel all running tasks
// 3. Find last completed checkpoint (e.g., CP-41)
// 4. Restore every operator's state from CP-41
// 5. Rewind sources to offsets stored in CP-41
// 6. Resume processing from consistent point
// Result: exactly-once state semantics
```

```text
Recovery timeline:
  CP-40 ──▶ CP-41 ──▶ [processing...] ──▶ CRASH
                ▲
         Last completed checkpoint
         Restore all operators to CP-41 state
         Rewind Kafka offsets to CP-41 positions
         Resume processing
```

### What about exactly-once end-to-end?

Checkpointing alone guarantees consistent state, not consistent external side effects. Exactly-once end-to-end additionally requires transactional or idempotent sinks.

```java
// State exactly-once: guaranteed by checkpointing
// End-to-end exactly-once: requires transactional sink

// Example: two-phase commit Kafka sink
import org.apache.flink.streaming.connectors.kafka.FlinkKafkaProducer;

FlinkKafkaProducer<String> kafkaSink = new FlinkKafkaProducer<>(
    "nbi-enriched-txns",
    new SimpleStringSchema(),
    properties,
    FlinkKafkaProducer.Semantic.EXACTLY_ONCE  // 2PC
);
stream.addSink(kafkaSink);
```

### What are the performance considerations?

Barrier alignment can cause backpressure when one input is slow, because fast channels are buffered while waiting. Asynchronous snapshots and incremental checkpointing (with RocksDB) keep checkpoint overhead low.

```java
// Monitor checkpoint metrics:
// - Checkpoint duration: time from trigger to completion
// - Alignment duration: time waiting for all barriers
// - State size: bytes uploaded per checkpoint
// - Checkpoints completed vs failed

// Configure timeouts
env.getCheckpointConfig().setCheckpointTimeout(60_000);  // 60s timeout
env.getCheckpointConfig().setMinPauseBetweenCheckpoints(5_000);  // 5s gap
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is not monitoring checkpoint duration. If checkpoints take longer than the interval, they overlap and eventually fail. Another mistake is forgetting that exactly-once state does not mean exactly-once side effects.

```java
// WRONG -- checkpoint interval shorter than checkpoint duration
env.enableCheckpointing(5_000);  // 5 seconds
// But state is 50 GB with full snapshots → takes 30 seconds
// Checkpoints overlap and fail!

// CORRECT -- use RocksDB incremental + adequate interval
env.setStateBackend(new EmbeddedRocksDBStateBackend(true));
env.enableCheckpointing(10_000);  // 10 seconds
// Incremental: uploads only delta, completes in seconds
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Barrier alignment is what guarantees a consistent cut across the distributed graph. Asynchronous snapshots keep the pipeline moving. Unaligned checkpoints (Flink 1.11+) exist to solve the backpressure amplification problem of alignment. And exactly-once end-to-end requires transactional or idempotent sinks beyond just checkpointing.
